{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by grant heslov, ben seveneves, and scott neustadter. the film is based on the true story of the founding of mark zuckerberg and his creation of facebook, the social networking site that revolutionized the way people interact and communicate.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a brilliant but awkward college student who creates facebook along with his college roommates and fellow harvard students edgar \"ed\" sutherland and adam \"jordan alford\" waley. the film also stars andrew garfield as sutherland and joel edgerton as tyler winklevoss, a twin brother who becomes embroiled in a lawsuit with zuckerberg over the rights to facebook.\n\nthe film explores the complexities of zuckerberg's character, revealing a brilliant but troubled young man who struggles to balance his academic and social life. eisenberg delivers a nuanced and compelling performance, capturing the essence of zuckerberg's intensity and passion for his creation.\n\nthe supporting cast is also impressive, with garfield bringing a sense of vulnerability and charisma to the role of sutherland, and edgerton delivering a chilling portrayal of the winklevoss twins. the film's cinematography is also noteworthy, capturing the dark and moody atmosphere of the harvard campus and the seedy underbelly of the tech industry.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its attention to detail, with the filmmakers taking great care to recreate the look and feel of the early days of facebook. the film's score, composed by todd field, is also a highlight, perfectly capturing the mood and tone of the film.\n\nthe film's script is also noteworthy, with the writers doing an excellent job of balancing the drama and suspense with the humor and wit. the film's pacing is also well-balanced, 